    <code code="10154-3" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="Chief complaint Narrative - Reported"/>
    <title>Chief Complaint</title>
    <text><paragraph>Fever and lower abdominal ache since last night, with a catheter in place after bladder surgery. [S]</paragraph></text>

  </section>
</component>

<component>
  <section>
    <templateId root="1.3.6.1.4.1.19376.1.5.3.1.3.4"/>
    <code code="10164-2" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="History of Present illness Narrative"/>
    <title>History of Present Illness</title>
    <text><paragraph>Voltaire is an 83-year-old man who had a transurethral resection of a bladder tumor five days ago and went home with an indwelling catheter. Since last night he has felt hot and shivery, with a dull ache above the pubic bone and cloudy urine in the bag; his temperature at home was 38.3 C. The urine has been light pink since the resection, without clots, and the catheter drains well. He has had no rigors, confusion, flank pain, vomiting, diarrhea, cough, or breathlessness, and he is drinking about 1.5 liters a day. He has not taken anything for the fever. He stopped the diphenhydramine sleep aid at his first visit and has taken nothing else to sleep. The resection pathology is not yet back. [S]</paragraph><table><thead><tr><th>Element</th><th>Detail</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Onset</td><td>Fever and suprapubic ache since last night, day 5 after resection</td></tr><tr><td>Catheter</td><td>16 Fr indwelling catheter since the resection, draining; urine cloudy and light pink</td></tr><tr><td>Pertinent negatives</td><td>No rigors, confusion, flank pain, vomiting, cough, or breathlessness</td></tr><tr><td>Medicines</td><td>Tamsulosin 0.4 mg and polyethylene glycol; no antibiotics since the single perioperative dose</td></tr></tbody></table></text>

  </section>
</component>

