    <code code="51847-2" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="Evaluation + Plan note"/>
    <title>Assessment and Plan</title>
    <text><list listType="ordered"><item><content styleCode="Bold">Febrile catheter-associated urinary tract infection (T83.511A)</content>. Fever, suprapubic pain, and pyuria (60 white cells per high-power field) with a white count of 12.6 and a catheter in place after resection: a symptomatic catheter-associated urinary tract infection without signs of sepsis or upper-tract infection. Creatinine 1.34 mg/dL, up slightly from 1.24. He can take tablets and fluids and has help at home, so oral treatment is reasonable. [S]<list><item>Catheter changed today, and the urine culture taken from the new catheter, as IDSA advises when a catheter has been in place more than two weeks or is part of the infection.</item><item>Cefpodoxime 200 mg twice daily for 7 days, adjusted to the culture; 7 days if the response is prompt.</item><item>Acetaminophen 1 g every 8 hours as needed for fever.</item><item>Drink 1.5 to 2 liters a day unless told otherwise; keep the bag below the bladder.</item><item>Telephone check tomorrow; go to the emergency department for rigors, confusion, vomiting, or fainting.</item></list></item><item><content styleCode="Bold">Visible blood in the urine (R31.0)</content>. Light pink urine without clots after resection; pathology pending. [S]<list><item>Pathology review at the visit in two days as planned.</item></list></item><item><content styleCode="Bold">Chronic urinary retention (R33.8)</content>. Catheter in place; the trial without catheter planned for the next visit will go ahead only if he is afebrile. [S]<list><item>Continue tamsulosin.</item></list></item></list><paragraph>Guideline basis: Management of Lower Urinary Tract Symptoms Attributed to Benign Prostatic Hyperplasia (BPH): AUA Guideline Amendment 2023 (2023); Diagnosis, Prevention, and Treatment of Catheter-Associated Urinary Tract Infection in Adults: 2009 International Clinical Practice Guidelines from the Infectious Diseases Society of America (2009)</paragraph><paragraph>Follow-up: Chronic-care visit in two days as planned, with the culture result; sooner for the warning signs above. Next scheduled visit 2026-04-22: review of resection pathology, diagnosis, geriatric assessment, trial without catheter, and shared decision on treatment. [S]</paragraph></text>

  </section>
